														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4824991">
									

<p>So, my question is this: does this SCP affect the relevant records on Earth anytime the SCP does its thing?</p>
<p>If so, you could attack this from the opposite angle: the SCP Foundation initially belives the SCP to be an event that affects sattleites and astronauts, ocasionally implanting both with fake memories or data about Earth, when what is really happening is the stone thing. The sattleites and astronauts are unaffected because the effects of the SCP are strictly Earthbound.</p>
<p>From there, you could then work your way to the narrative you had planned (perhaps the SCP being from a seperate investigation initially unrelated to the sattleite phenomenon?) I suggest this because, as-is, it would be difficult for the Foundation to &quot;naturally&quot; learn of this SCP's existence, and you've kinda-sorta created a plot hole by having the book binders suspect that the SCP is causing the stuff despite its effects on humanity's perception of history. Having them chase down the effects (even if they're incorrect) feels like a better way.</p>
<p>Also burning the bookstore in-universe is a bit much if the intent was to destroy the SCP. Sledgehammer or dropping it from a high place would've been the smater move for the guys.</p>

							</div>

								
					
