<p><strong>Disclaimer: I am a newbie and <span style="text-decoration: underline;">cannot</span> grant you greenlight.</strong></p>
<p>Hey there, Cesss. The idea itself is pretty unique, if nothing else. I've been following the SCP Foundation for a couple of years and I don't think I ever read about a Gas station being an SCP before. I could also see a couple possible options for a good interview being made out of the narrative you have provided, which is always nice to see.</p>
<p>Now, I would have a question, if I may: does this SCP seek out Foundation personnel to make into it's victims on it's own? Because while I know that Foundation personnel are the most likely to die at the hands of SCPs, I find it peculiar in your article.</p>
<p>You said the Researcher was driving home from work, meaning he was leaving a Site or Facility of the Foundation. I would be of the opinion that the Foundation keeps a good patrol around their Facilities, taking in consideration what they work with.</p>
<p>Next, you said that this SCP swaps the real person with the reflection. I take it then, that the Researcher was trapped in a pocket dimension within the mirror, looking at the reflection which was now controlling his body? Then, if they both swung a hammer at each other, which can only inflict bludgeoning wounds, why did the Research die of multiple lacerations?</p>
<p>Another question I would have: how would this SCP be contained?</p>
<p>Lastly, there are a couple of SCPs with <span style="text-decoration: underline;">reflection</span> as their main selling point on the side. Currently I can think of SCP-125, and SCP-1620. As the author of your SCP, you know the exact details to your article, so I would recommend you looking up any other SCPs like 125 and 1620 and check for similarities, in order to avoid your article being too similar to already existing ones.</p>
<p>With that said, I kind of really like your article here. With a bit of fine-tuning and a bit more highlight into the <span style="text-decoration: underline;">swapping process</span>, I could see this getting somewhere.</p>
<p>Again, please remember that I am a newbie. Take everything I said with a grain of salt.</p>

